class:: VelvetFDN
summary:: Sparse-diffused FDN with optional paraunitary filtered feedback
categories:: UGens>Reverbs, Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN, Guides/CamaraObscura

description::

image::../Guides/ir_velvetfdn.png#VelvetFDN shows an immediate, thick block of solid density that bypasses the slow build-up of traditional FDNs by embedding velvet sequences directly into the feedback loops.::

Conceptually, `VelvetFDN` merges two powerful reverberation strategies: the smooth, echo-free density of velvet noise, and the infinite sustain of a Feedback Delay Network (FDN). Traditional FDNs can sometimes sound metallic or have audible repeating echoes before the echo density sufficiently builds up. By incorporating velvet noise, this reverb achieves a thick, lush tail instantly upon excitation.

Technically, `VelvetFDN` replaces the standard delay lines in an FDN with sparse, randomized velvet noise FIR filters. To maintain strict mathematical stability (losslessness) in the feedback loop when using non-ideal FIR filters, it employs an advanced paraunitary structure—a Hadamard–delay–Hadamard matrix formulation. This ensures the UGen remains unconditionally stable and artifact-free even with extreme decay times, offering a uniquely colored, rapidly-diffusing reverberation block.

VelvetFDN uses fixed deterministic velvet FIR taps to accelerate echo-density
growth around a conventional FDN, or uses the prepared paraunitary
code::H D(z) H:: structure inside feedback. The latter was verified on a
1024-point frequency grid to numerical precision and is lossless before
two-band attenuation.

Output is stereo and wet only. code::delayCount:: is fixed at construction and
must match the model. Sparse histories and all main delays are fixed RT
allocations made during construction. CPU increases with lines and active
taps. code::density:: selects the active fraction of prepared taps and
code::diffusion:: crossfades direct versus sparse-filtered values; both may be
audio-rate but fast modulation can sound granular.

Freeze removes excitation and moves attenuation toward a stable 0.999999
delay-loop gain. Reset clears FDN, sparse FIR, and paraunitary delay states.
The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/AllpassN:: used as a diffuser;
VelvetFDN uses a deterministic sparse topology and optional proven
paraunitary feedback rather than a scalar allpass cascade.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: input
Mono audio input.

argument:: modelBuf
Loaded link::Classes/VelvetFDNModel:: Buffer.

argument:: delayCount
Literal line count from 2 through 32.

argument:: density
Active sparse-tap fraction from 0 through 1. At least one tap remains active.

argument:: diffusion
Crossfade from undiffused to normalized sparse-filter output.

argument:: freeze
Stable near-lossless hold from 0 through 1.

argument:: reset
Rising edge clears every recursive and sparse-filter state.

returns::
Stereo wet audio Array.

method:: kr
Unavailable; audio-rate only.

examples::

The following examples have been structured into individual blocks so you can easily run them step-by-step.

code::
// Boot the server first!
s.boot;

// 1. Prepare the model and source material
(
// Use a fallback to ExampleFiles.child if the external sample bundle is not installed
~path = Platform.userExtensionDir +/+ "CamaraObscura/sounds/";
~load = { |name| if(File.exists(~path +/+ name)) { ~path +/+ name } { ExampleFiles.child } };

~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("vocal.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum1.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("drum2.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("guitar.wav"), channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~load.value("violin.wav"), channels: [0]);

~model = VelvetFDNModel.default(8, 3, s.sampleRate).load(s);
)

// 2. Play the reverb (wait a moment for the buffers to load before executing this)
(
x = {
    var input = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~source, BufRateScale.kr(~source), loop: 1);
    // var input = SoundIn.ar(0); // alternative live input
    
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.12]));
    var wet = VelvetFDN.ar(input, ~model)[0];
    
    (input + wet) * fade * 0.25 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
x.free;
~source.free;
~model.free;
::

// Creative Example: Pumping the dense tail with heavy compression
code::
// 1. Load the model
~creativeModel = ~creativeModel = VelvetFDNModel.default(12, 4, s.sampleRate).load(s);

// 2. Play
(
y = {
    var denseWet = VelvetFDN.ar(Dust.ar(4), ~creativeModel)[0];
    Compander.ar(denseWet, denseWet, 0.1, 1, 0.1, 0.01, 0.1) * 3
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
y.free;
~creativeModel.free;
::
